\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{representation-theory}

\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Triality cycles three real modules and preserves the trilinear}

\p{For the automorphism #{\tau} of \ref{fcap-003T}, one can choose
real forms of #{S^+,S^-}, invariant positive inner products on them,
and real isometries

##{J_0:V_{\mathbb R}\longrightarrow S^+_{\mathbb R},\qquad
J_1:S^+_{\mathbb R}\longrightarrow S^-_{\mathbb R},\qquad
J_2:S^-_{\mathbb R}\longrightarrow V_{\mathbb R}}

whose cyclic direct-sum map #{J} has #{J^3=1}. Their complex-linear
extensions preserve exactly the trilinear #{F} of \ref{fcap-003D}:

##{F(J_2s_-,J_0v,J_1s_+)=F(v,s_+,s_-).}

The chosen forward cycle intertwines the diagonal group action with
#{\tau^{-1}}. The real forms and inner products are specified in the
construction below.}

\subtree{
\taxon{Proof}
\p{Let #{E=\mathbb R^8} with its Euclidean inner product, and let
#{\pi:G\to SO(E)} be the vector action. On three copies of this
same real space define the twisted modules

##{V_i=(E,\pi\circ\tau^i),\qquad i\in\mathbb Z/3\mathbb Z.}

Their complexifications have classes #{V,S^+,S^-}, respectively,
by \ref{fcap-003T}. Thus \ref{fcap-003D} gives a one-dimensional
complex space of invariant trilinears on their complexifications.}

\p{The invariance equations on #{E^{\otimes3}} have real
coefficients. The real and imaginary parts of every invariant complex
trilinear are therefore invariant real trilinears. It follows that

##{L=(V_0^*\otimes_{\mathbb R}V_1^*\otimes_{\mathbb R}V_2^*)^G,
\qquad \dim_{\mathbb R}L=1,}

because complexification identifies #{L\otimes_{\mathbb R}\mathbb C}
with the invariant complex space. Choose #{0\ne t\in L}.}

\p{Write

##{D(g)=\operatorname{diag}\bigl(\pi(g),\pi(\tau g),
\pi(\tau^2g)\bigr).}

Define #{Ct(x_0,x_1,x_2)=t(x_1,x_2,x_0)} using the common
underlying space #{E}. Permuting #{D(g)(x_0,x_1,x_2)} this way
gives #{D(\tau g)(x_1,x_2,x_0)}, so #{C} preserves #{L}.
Moreover #{C^3=1}. On a real one-dimensional space this means
#{C=c} for a real scalar with #{c^3=1}; hence #{c=1}. In particular,

##{t(x_0,x_1,x_2)=t(x_1,x_2,x_0)=t(x_2,x_0,x_1).}

The real field is essential here: over #{\mathbb C}, a cube-one
scalar need not be one.}

\p{Choose complex #{G}-module isomorphisms

##{\phi_0=1:V_{0,\mathbb C}\to V,\qquad
\phi_1:V_{1,\mathbb C}\to S^+,\qquad
\phi_2:V_{2,\mathbb C}\to S^-.}

They exist by the proved representation comparisons. The pullback of
#{F} along these maps is #{\lambda t_{\mathbb C}} for some
#{\lambda\ne0}. Multiplying #{\phi_1} by a unit complex scalar
makes #{\lambda} real. Now define
#{S^+_{\mathbb R}=\phi_1(E)} and
#{S^-_{\mathbb R}=\phi_2(E)}, with inner products transported from
#{E}. They are #{G}-invariant real forms with positive inner products,
and the restriction of #{F} to these three real forms is real-valued.}

\p{Set

##{J_0=\phi_1\phi_0^{-1},\qquad
J_1=\phi_2\phi_1^{-1},\qquad
J_2=\phi_0\phi_2^{-1}.}

In these formulas the underlying copies of #{E} are identified;
the maps relate differently twisted actions. Each is a real isometry
for the stated metrics, and each cyclic composite of all three is the
identity. With #{\Phi=\phi_0\oplus\phi_1\oplus\phi_2}, let
#{J=\Phi J_E\Phi^{-1}}, where
#{J_E(x_0,x_1,x_2)=(x_2,x_0,x_1)}. Thus #{J^3=1}.}

\p{The diagonal action on #{V\oplus S^+\oplus S^-} is
#{\mathcal D(g)=\Phi D(g)\Phi^{-1}}. In the common coordinates,

##{J_E D(g)=D(\tau^{-1}g)J_E,}

so #{J\mathcal D(g)=\mathcal D(\tau^{-1}g)J}. Finally the cyclic
identity for #{t} gives

##{\begin{aligned}
F(J_2s_-,J_0v,J_1s_+)
&=\lambda t_{\mathbb C}(x_2,x_0,x_1)\\
&=\lambda t_{\mathbb C}(x_0,x_1,x_2)
=F(v,s_+,s_-),
\end{aligned}}

where #{v=\phi_0x_0}, #{s_+=\phi_1x_1}, and #{s_-=\phi_2x_2}.
No rescaling of #{F} was made, so its top-form normalization in
\ref{fcap-003D} is retained.}
}

\p{Proposition 5.7 in \citet{pp. 35--36}{adams1996exceptional}
supplies the invariant-line and real-valued trilinear statements.
Exercises 20.50--20.51 in
\citet{pp. 314--315}{fulton2004representation} describe the related
cyclic tensor symmetry. The construction above obtains that symmetry
from the chosen compact automorphism and the real invariant line.
Its isometry claim uses the transported inner products just defined;
it does not identify them with preselected exterior bilinear forms or
assert a normed composition algebra.}
